    "text": "Cauldrons rank among the most treasured objects in Irish and Welsh story. Late Bronze Age examples from about 1000 to 500 BC survive across Ireland and Britain. Some were left as votive gifts, others buried at Hallstatt sites. The Gundestrup cauldron belongs to this group. In story the cauldron stands for plenty and for rebirth. In Branwen a cauldron of rebirth revives dead warriors. Ceridwen's cauldron grants wisdom, and its potion gives Gwion the knowledge that makes him Taliesin. The chief words for such a vessel in the Celtic tongues descend from one old root, the coire of Ireland and Scotland and the peir of medieval Welsh. In the tale of the raid on Tara, Cormac mac Airt is made to pass beneath the wooden fork that held a cauldron, a sign of submission. Some cauldrons read the truth of a matter by the boiling of their meat.",
    "okr": {
        "objective": "Document the cauldron as a prehistoric artefact and as a central symbol in Celtic story.",
